From: Luma contributors
Subject: [PATCH] gallivm: propagate detected host features on AArch64

LLVM reports negative optional host features such as -sve on AArch64.
Pass those detected features to Gallivm as already done for 32-bit Arm,
so selecting the host CPU does not re-enable instructions unavailable to
the running kernel. Keep CPU selection and all feature detection intact.

Use the iterator returned by features.begin() so the same loop remains
compatible with LLVM 22, which no longer exposes StringMapIterator.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Source: Mesa 26.1.6, MIT-licensed Gallivm source

--- a/src/gallium/auxiliary/gallivm/lp_bld_misc.cpp
+++ b/src/gallium/auxiliary/gallivm/lp_bld_misc.cpp
@@ -317,7 +317,7 @@
 lp_build_fill_mattrs(std::vector<std::string> &MAttrs)
 {
 
-#if DETECT_ARCH_ARM
+#if DETECT_ARCH_ARM || DETECT_ARCH_AARCH64
    /* llvm-3.3+ implements sys::getHostCPUFeatures for Arm,
     * which allows us to enable/disable code generation based
     * on the results of cpuid on these architectures.
@@ -331,7 +331,7 @@
       llvm::sys::getHostCPUFeatures(features);
    #endif
 
-   for (llvm::StringMapIterator<bool> f = features.begin();
+   for (auto f = features.begin();
         f != features.end();
         ++f) {
       MAttrs.push_back(((*f).second ? "+" : "-") + (*f).first().str());
